<HelpLayout title="Progress tracking" description="How personal records work in Badger, and how to read exercise graphs, strength standards, plateau detection, weekly volume and muscle balance.">
  <h1>Progress tracking</h1>

  <p>The Progress tab has two tabs: <strong>Exercises</strong>, for one exercise's records and graphs, and <strong>Overview</strong>, for your training as a whole. This page is also where personal records are explained.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Find an exercise</h2>
  <p>The Exercises tab lists every exercise, most recently trained first. Tap the sort icon to switch to A to Z. The search bar and filter chips work the same way as in the exercise library. Tap an exercise to open its progress screen.</p>

  <h2 id="prs">What counts as a personal record</h2>
  <p>A working set that beats your previous best is a personal record (PR). Badger marks the set with a trophy badge and shows a brief notice at the bottom of the training screen. Every exercise type has records, and what counts depends on the type:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Weight + reps</strong> - the highest <strong>estimated 1RM</strong>, using the Epley formula: <code>weight × (1 + reps / 30)</code>. More weight, or more reps at the same weight, is a PR.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight only</strong> - heaviest weight.</li>
    <li><strong>Reps only</strong> - most reps.</li>
    <li><strong>Distance</strong> - farthest distance.</li>
    <li><strong>Time</strong> - the longest time, unless you set <a href={`#record-time`}>Record time</a> to Shorter.</li>
    <li><strong>Combined types</strong> (for example distance + time) - going farther, or covering the same distance faster, each counts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Warmup sets never count, and neither do <a href="/help/gyms/#accessories-and-records">sets logged with an accessory</a> such as a band or chains.</p>
  <p><strong>Records are kept per machine and per bar.</strong> With a machine or a <a href="/help/gyms/#bar-prs">bar</a> set on the exercise, a set is compared only with sets on that same machine and bar. With neither set, it's compared with all your history.</p>
  <p><strong>Linked exercises share records.</strong> If you've <a href="/help/exercises/#linking">linked two exercises</a>, a new best on either counts as a PR on both, and their graphs combine.</p>

  <h3>Timed and assisted exercises</h3>
  <p id="record-time"><strong>Choose whether a longer or shorter time wins.</strong> On a timed exercise's edit screen, set <strong>Record time</strong> to <em>Longer</em> (holds, carries, dead hangs), <em>Shorter</em> (timed runs and for-time efforts) or <em>Default</em>. Default means longer, except for distance + time exercises, where faster wins.</p>

  <p id="record-weight"><strong>Assisted exercises: set Record weight to Lighter.</strong> On an assisted pull-up or dip machine, the weight you select takes weight off you, so a lower number is the harder set. On the exercise's edit screen, set <strong>Record weight</strong> to <em>Lighter</em> so less assistance counts as the record. <em>Assisted Pull-Up</em> comes set up this way.</p>
  <p>These exercises show a <strong>Lightest</strong> best instead of an estimated 1RM, and they get no weight-increase or warmup suggestions.</p>

  <h2>An exercise's progress screen</h2>
  <p>The screen has two tabs: <strong>Overview</strong> (stats, graph and records) and <strong>History</strong> (every session, with sets you can edit). History is covered on the History page under <a href={`/help/history/#per-exercise`}>One exercise's history</a>.</p>
  <p>The stats row at the top shows:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Estimated 1RM</strong> - your current best.</li>
    <li><strong>30-day change</strong> - how much your estimated 1RM has moved over the last 30 days, as a percentage.</li>
    <li><strong>Last session</strong> - the date and a short summary of the last time you did it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="graphs">Graphs</h2>
  <p>Tap the chips above the chart to switch what it plots:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Estimated 1RM</strong> - best estimated 1RM per session.</li>
    <li><strong>Max weight</strong> - heaviest weight per session.</li>
    <li><strong>Workout volume</strong> - total weight × reps per session.</li>
    <li><strong>Total reps</strong> - reps across all working sets per session.</li>
    <li><strong>Max reps</strong> - most reps in one set per session.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight at N reps</strong> - heaviest weight at a chosen rep count.</li>
    <li><strong>Rep maxes</strong> - best weight for each rep count over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Max distance / Total distance / Max time / Total time / Max speed / Max pace</strong> - for distance and timed exercises.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Graph controls</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Data points</strong> - show or hide the markers.</li>
    <li><strong>Trend line</strong> - add a straight trend line.</li>
    <li><strong>Y from 0</strong> - start the vertical axis at zero.</li>
    <li><strong>Share</strong> - send the chart as an image through the share sheet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Tap a point to see its values below the chart, and use the arrows to step from point to point. Tap the values to open that session's workout summary.</p>

  <h3 id="chart-filters">Chart filters</h3>
  <p>Filter rows appear above the chart once your history has something to filter:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Machine</strong> - if you've done the exercise on more than one machine. Pick one, or <strong>All</strong> to combine them. The machine you used last is selected to start with.</li>
    <li><strong>Bar</strong> - if you've used two or more named bars. It starts on <strong>All bars</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Accessories</strong> - if your history mixes plain sets with sets using bands, chains or straps: <strong>All work</strong>, <strong>No accessories</strong> or <strong>Accessories only</strong>. In All work, sessions with accessories appear as hollow rings. They never count as <a href="/help/gyms/#accessories-and-records">records</a>, so the BEST line can sit below a ring, and Accessories only shows no record marks at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="strength-standards">Strength standards</h2>
  <p>Badger can draw reference lines on the estimated 1RM graph showing where a lift sits for your bodyweight, from untrained through novice, intermediate and advanced to elite. They cover five barbell lifts: <strong>bench press, squat, deadlift, overhead press and barbell row</strong>. Only exercises whose name matches one of those get them; dumbbell and machine versions aren't covered.</p>

  <h3>Turn them on</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open one of those lifts' progress screen, on the estimated 1RM graph.</li>
    <li>Tap the <strong>Standards</strong> chip below the graph.</li>
    <li>The first time, answer the setup questions (below).</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Standards are set per exercise, so you can turn them on only for the lifts you're chasing a number on.</p>

  <h3>What it asks you</h3>
  <p>Standards differ by sex, so Badger asks for yours. Change or remove it in <strong>Settings &rarr; Coaching &rarr; Strength standards</strong>; choosing <strong>Prefer not to say</strong> turns the standards off.</p>
  <p>The levels are multiples of your bodyweight, taken from your <a href="/help/body/">body tracker</a>. If you've never logged a weigh-in, setup asks for your weight and saves it there as an ordinary entry. Log new weigh-ins as usual and the lines follow.</p>

  <h3>Reading the lines</h3>
  <p>Only the levels near your data are drawn: the next level up appears once it's within reach. Your all-time best keeps its own darker line. The records list below the graph adds a line with your current level and your best estimated 1RM as a multiple of bodyweight, for example <em>Intermediate &middot; est 1RM 1.4x BW</em>.</p>
  <p>Treat the lines as a rough guide. Badger's table combines several public strength references, and it uses flat multiples of bodyweight, while real standards shift with body weight and age.</p>

  <h2 id="plateau-detection">Plateau detection</h2>
  <p>Plateau detection is <strong>off by default</strong>. Turn it on in <strong>Settings → Coaching → Plateau detection</strong>. Once it's on, Badger shows a banner on an exercise's progress screen when its estimated 1RM has stalled.</p>
  <p>A plateau is flagged when <strong>both</strong> are true:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your estimated 1RM trend is flat or falling (rising less than 0.5% per session).</li>
    <li>The sessions it looks at span at least 3 weeks.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The banner says how long the stall has lasted and suggests three things:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A deload week, with a shortcut to your deload settings.</li>
    <li>A different rep range (for example 4-6 for a few weeks if you've been doing 8-10).</li>
    <li>A variation that works the same muscles from a different angle.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The Today screen also shows a card with up to two plateauing exercises. Tap a name to open its progress screen.</p>
  <p><strong>Sensitivity</strong>, on the same settings screen, sets how many sessions it looks at:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conservative</strong> - about 10 sessions of flat progress.</li>
    <li><strong>Balanced</strong> (default) - about 8.</li>
    <li><strong>Sensitive</strong> - about 6.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It works only on weighted exercises (barbell, dumbbell and so on), because it reads estimated 1RM.</p>

  <h2>Overview tab</h2>
  <p>The Overview tab covers all your training at once.</p>

  <h3 id="weekly-volume">Weekly volume</h3>
  <p>The <strong>Volume</strong> card shows how many sets you've done per muscle group in the current window: 7 days, or your active routine's cycle length if it has one. Each group is a bar in its category color, with the set count colored by zone. Groups with no sets are hidden.</p>

  <h4>Volume zones</h4>
  <p>Each bar's background shows three zones, explained in a small legend on the card:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Amber</strong> - below MEV (minimum effective volume): too little to drive progress.</li>
    <li><strong>Green</strong> - between MEV and MRV: the productive range.</li>
    <li><strong>Red</strong> - above MRV (maximum recoverable volume): more than you can recover from before the next session.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Weekly targets</h4>
  <p>Tap <strong>⚙</strong> in the card header to set a target for any muscle group. It shows as a tick on the bar and a <code>/target</code> after the set count. Leave a group blank to use the zones alone.</p>

  <h3>Activity card</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Training frequency</strong> - workouts per week, as a bar chart.</li>
    <li><strong>Activity heatmap</strong> - 26 weeks as a grid of squares, one per day, shaded by how much you did. Switch between <strong>Sets</strong> (sets not skipped), <strong>Volume</strong> (weight × reps, converted to kg) and <strong>Duration</strong> (session minutes).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="muscle-balance">Muscle balance</h3>
  <p>Badger tracks how often you train each muscle group and flags the ones falling behind. On the Today screen, a colored dot on a weekly volume chip marks a group that needs attention.</p>
  <p>When any group is flagged, a <strong>Muscle balance</strong> card appears on the Overview tab. For each group it shows the status (OK, warning or neglected), when you last trained it, and its set count in the rolling window. Tap <strong>+ Log exercise</strong> on a flagged group to open the exercise picker filtered to that category.</p>
  <p>To turn the warnings off, or mute single categories, go to <strong>Settings → Coaching → Muscle balance warnings</strong>.</p>
</HelpLayout>
